Starting from a filter of the system of finitely generated subsystems, reconstructing its subsystem and then inducing the filter of that subsystem returns the original filter.

Compare filters by the neighbourhoods they confirm. A neighbourhood confirmed by the induced filter has a generating list whose basic neighbourhoods lie in the reconstructed subsystem, so those neighbourhoods have a common witness already confirmed by the original filter, and upward closure along the reversed inclusion of generated neighbourhoods returns the neighbourhood. Conversely, a neighbourhood confirmed by the original filter is generated by some list, and each basic neighbourhood of that list lies in the reconstructed subsystem because it belongs to the finitely generated family of the list.
